    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Show that $y=3x^2$ is increasing for $x>0$ and decreasing for $x<0$. What happens at $x=0$?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$y'=6x$. For $x>0$, $y'>0$, so the curve is increasing. For $x<0$, $y'<0$, so the curve is decreasing. At $x=0$, $y'=0$, so the curve is stationary.",
      "worked": "$$y'=6x$$\nFor $x>0$, $6x>0$, so the curve is increasing.\n\nFor $x<0$, $6x<0$, so the curve is decreasing.\n\nAt $x=0$, $y'=6(0)=0$, so the curve has a stationary point.",
